WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
this week as we marked one year since  the pandemic was declared it's no secret  that nursing homes  got it the worst a mixture of older  residents in limited space created a  perfect hunting ground for the deadliest  pandemic in more than 100 years  it was especially bad in the beginning  but now one year in the question is have  nursing homes made any real changes or  any attempts  to improve after the crisis we set out  to investigate she hated it  the first week she hated it there the  food  the staff members she said they weren't  nice  to the patients last october after a  fall  inside their home don eshelman's wife  sue was sent to the hospital  with a broken ankle with don working  overnights and unable to care for sue  around the clock  the couple decided the best option was  for her to do her rehab work  at the gardens at stevens a nursing home  in rural pennsylvania  and how did you meet we bet on american  online  really yes a chat room don and sue were  married in 2001.  at the time of her fall sue at 69 was  fighting kidney cancer and diabetes  but don says both were under control the  plan was for her to return home  in time for thanksgiving but not long  after she arrived  seven months after the pandemic first  began  the coronavirus swept through the  gardens at stevens  sioux along with about 80 percent of the  other residents at the home  tested positive she was eventually sent  to the hospital  wednesday they called me now this is a  wednesday before thanksgiving  they had called me and told me that they  had to put her on a ventilator  on the morning of december second don  says he got a call saying sue  should go to hospice he got a second  call that night  physician taking care of her called me  around six seven o'clock at night  told me that she's not even going to  make it to hospice what do i want  to do  make her as comfortable as possible i  don't want her to feel it  feel any pain  10 30 i get a phone call from the  hospital telling me that she had passed  sue was one of 39 patients who died from  that outbreak  two-thirds of the residents at the  nursing home  don eshelman has now begun legal action  against the gardens at stevens  he wants to know why they weren't better  prepared for a second wave  was it neglect on their part were they  short staffed  i don't know were they having their  employees come to work sick  and working i don't know  the home hasn't responded to you at all  no  they sent me a card  telling me how sorry they were for my  loss  one year and now to the worst pandemic  in a century  have nursing homes made significant  improvements  no matt yarnell is president of  sciu pennsylvania the union representing  health care workers across the  commonwealth we've had a  decades-old problem in pennsylvania  nursing homes and nursing homes around  our country but i think  you know given the covid you know crisis  it lay bear you know really decades of  problems  in the industry what's the biggest  problem i would say  the lack of staff currently nursing  homes in pennsylvania  are required to devote 2.7 hours of  personal care to each patient  in a 24-hour period a number that hasn't  changed since the 1990s  even as the population has aged less  than three hours of care  for somebody who's oftentimes totally  dependent  so they need bathed fed you know  changed toileted you know you name it  everything that we do every day to you  know sort of have basic  activities of daily living folks need  those supports and trying to figure out  how to do that  with you know in 2.7 hours of care in a  24 hour period is not very much at all  yarnell wants the amount of required  daily care to move from 2.7  to 4.1 one obstacle more staff to make  that happen means  more costs 68 of the nursing homes in  the us are  profit lower staffing levels mean higher  profits for investors  and that may have contributed to the  high levels of deaths in u.s nursing  homes from the coveted crisis  several recent studies have found that  low staffing levels were linked to large  outbreaks in individual nursing homes  another issue accountability who  actually owns the nursing homes and  how's the money spent  most of it taxpayer-funded owners listed  in public records for the gardens of  stevens  are connected to a total of over 200  other nursing homes  including the ones stan lee dawson just  retired from  you know once you get used to one set of  management  and next thing you know they make enough  money where  you know they take it out of the game  and they pass it on to the next  management company dawson worked in  nursing homes for more than 30 years  at his last one care pavilion he says  ownership passed from  one group to another three times in 16  years  when the homes got sold did the care  ever get any better  nah the the best time i can remember  from my years of working there was when  i first started  that was 2004. the  company that was running everything at  that time  was a a non-profit  the ones that came behind them  became profit-making companies did you  get the sense that owners cared about  the workers no did you get the sense  that the owners cared about  the residents somewhat  you know not like they should somewhat  you've got 60 beds  in the current your current facility how  many nursing assistants  the ratio for 60 about three how much  time does that allow you to spend with  patients individually on a daily basis  very very little  julie moore has been a nursing assistant  for more than 20 years  she works at the same nursing home in  philadelphia that dawson did  and met us before a recent shift why  won't they hire more people  they've hired people but they still  don't staff the floors correctly and  when we go to management about it they  the reason  is their their budget they're over  budget is the reason that we're getting  are you scared to speak out no not at  all  not worried about your job no  why is it so important for you to do  this because it's just not fair  the industry left to itself to correct  itself is not going to work  ultimately you're talking about money  yes but but with clear outcomes  that we're going to invest in making  sure there are enough people to do the  job and do it well  so getting to a minimum staffing level  across this country that is humane  many people already struggle big time  trying to pay for care for their parents  who are in nursing homes  you're asking them to pay more now i'm  not advocating for blank checks i'm  advocating for  you know minimum standards that are  standards that are  that are really built around care and  also transparency on where the dollars  go so we actually know what we're paying  for  have you been given any kind of timeline  don echelman has been asking to see his  wife's records from the gardens at  stevens  for three months so far to no avail  what answers do you want from the home  i want to know why  i mean it's not been the pandemic  distance started in november this  pandemic started back in march do you  think you'll get those answers  i'm hoping i get them answers that's all  i'm asking for is what happened  how did how did 39 families  lose their loved ones  so there's 38 other families out there  probably wondering the same thing i'm  wondering  what happened we reached out to the  gardens at stevens and care pavilion  multiple times for comment as well as  the owners  don ashelman is seeking damages from  they did not have  a comment and what is what is more  staffing means it means that there's  more people to do  procedures the right way it also means  the workers aren't rushed  and overworked and can pay more  attention to the patients  it seems like when that profit model is  first  that's where people start to suffer and  if they haven't learned  whether it's a pandemic or something  else there has to be better care  i think we're going to learn a lot more  in the coming years as we look at what  happened over the past year

